/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A chain is 100 or 150 links of galvanised steel wire, each link 0.2 m, with a brass handle at each end; a metric chain is 20 m or 30 m.
   - Ranging is fixing intermediate points on the straight line between two stations.
   - Reconnaissance comes first: walk the site, note buildings, trees and the road, and sketch an index map showing the stations.
   - An offset is a short measurement from the chain line out to an object — fence corner, tree, manhole.
   - Every tape has built-in error, and field conditions differ from the day it was standardized.
   - standardization: L′ true length between end marks (m), L nominal length (30 m), M measured length (m).
   - temperature: α is the coefficient of expansion, about 11 to 12 × 10⁻⁶ per °C for steel (use the value the question gives), T field temperature (°C), T₀ standardization temperature (°C).
   - tension: P applied pull (N), P₀ standard pull (N), A tape cross-section (mm²), E Young's modulus, 2.0 × 10⁵ N/mm² for steel.
   - sag, for one span: w weight per metre (N/m), l unsupported span (m), P pull (N).
   - slope and alignment: h level difference between ends (m), or distance tape is held off the line (m).
*/
