/* book Worked example: Q: A 30 m steel tape was standardized at 20 °C. It is used at 35 °C to measure a line, and the reading is 30.000 m. The coefficient of thermal expansion of steel is 11.2 × 10⁻⁶ per °C. (a) Find the temperature correction and the true length. (b) A separate slope measurement between two stations is 20.000 m along the slope, with a level difference of 1.200 m between the ends. Find the horizontal distance and the slope correction.
   1. Temperature difference: ΔT = 35 − 20 = 15 °C.
   2. Temperature correction: ct = M α ΔT = 30.000 × 11.2 × 10⁻⁶ × 15 = 0.00504 m = +5.04 mm.
   3. True length = M + ct = 30.000 + 0.00504 = 30.00504 m ≈ 30.005 m.
   4. Horizontal distance = √(M² − h²) = √(20.000² − 1.200²) = √(400.000 − 1.440) = √398.560 = 19.964 m.
   5. Slope correction = horizontal − measured = 19.964 − 20.000 = −0.036 m.
   Answer: Temperature correction = +5.04 mm, giving a true length of 30.005 m; the horizontal distance is 19.964 m, a slope correction of −0.036 m. */
