/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An electromagnetic wave travels through air at speed v, close to 3×10⁸ m/s.
   - d is the one-way distance in m; v is the wave speed in air in m/s; Δt is the total there-and-back travel time in s.
   - Most survey EDM uses phase difference.
   - d is the one-way distance; λ = v/f is the modulation wavelength in air; N is the whole number of cycles in the double path; Δφ is the phase difference in radians.
   - λ = v/f is the modulation wavelength in air.
   - EDM instruments are also grouped by the carrier wave.
   - Typical prism EDM range is a few hundred metres to several kilometres.
   - σd is the expected error in mm; a is the constant part in mm; b is the distance-dependent part in ppm; d is the measured distance in km.
   - The constant part covers instrument zero error and phase resolution.
   - Operation: set the instrument on the station with a tribrach and level it; set the prism on the target station; enter prism constant, temperature and pressure; aim at the prism; press measure; record slope distance.
*/
